    "steps": [
      {
        "text": "Scrape any hairs off the pork skin with a knife. Cover the pork with water, bring to a boil and boil until scum rises. Drain and rinse.",
        "minutes": 10
      },
      {
        "text": "Return the pork to the pot with the 2.5 liters of water, onion, bay leaves and salt. Bring to a boil, skim, cover and simmer until the skin is soft and the meat is fork-tender.",
        "minutes": 105
      },
      {
        "text": "While the pork cooks, toast the rice in a dry pan until deep golden, cool and grind fine. Rub and strain the annatto seeds, keeping the orange water.",
        "minutes": null
      },
      {
        "text": "Lift out the pork. Measure 5 cups of the cooking liquid and skim off most of the fat.",
        "minutes": null
      },
      {
        "text": "Blanch the eggplant and string beans in the remaining liquid for about 3 minutes and the bok choy for 1 minute. Drain.",
        "minutes": 4
      },
      {
        "text": "Heat the oil in a wide pot and saute the garlic and onion until soft. Add the annatto water, peanut butter, ground peanuts and the 5 cups of liquid, and whisk until smooth.",
        "minutes": null
      },
      {
        "text": "Stir the ground rice into 1/2 cup water, add it to the sauce and simmer, stirring often, until thick.",
        "minutes": 10
      },
      {
        "text": "Return the pork to the sauce and simmer to heat through. Season lightly with fish sauce.",
        "minutes": 8
      },
      {
        "text": "Arrange the vegetables over the pork and serve with rice and sauteed shrimp paste.",
        "minutes": null
      }
    ],
    "tips": "Have the butcher saw the hock into rounds for you; home knives will not get through the bone. Front hocks are meatier than back hocks."
