    "steps": [
      {
        "text": "Stir 1 tablespoon of vinegar into the blood and keep it in the fridge until needed. If it has started to clot, press it through a sieve.",
        "minutes": null
      },
      {
        "text": "Heat the oil in a heavy pot over medium heat. Saute the garlic and onion until soft.",
        "minutes": null
      },
      {
        "text": "Add the pork and cook, stirring, until it loses its pink color and some fat renders. Season with the fish sauce and cook until the pork starts to brown.",
        "minutes": 8
      },
      {
        "text": "Add the water and bay leaves, bring to a boil, then cover and simmer until the pork is tender.",
        "minutes": 35
      },
      {
        "text": "Pour in the 1/2 cup vinegar and boil <strong>without stirring</strong> so the raw sharpness cooks off.",
        "minutes": 3
      },
      {
        "text": "Lower the heat to medium-low. Pour in the blood in a thin stream while stirring constantly so it does not curdle into lumps.",
        "minutes": null
      },
      {
        "text": "Keep stirring and simmer until the sauce darkens and thickens to a gravy. Add the chilies and sugar in the last few minutes.",
        "minutes": 12
      },
      {
        "text": "Season with salt and pepper. Remove the bay leaves and serve hot with rice or puto.",
        "minutes": null
      }
    ],
    "tips": "Buy the blood the same day you cook it and keep it cold. Constant stirring after the blood goes in is what keeps the sauce smooth."
